  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Script Errors**\n\nI\'ve been examining the causes of \"Script error.\" messages. It seems the browser obscures error details when unhandled errors or errors in third-party scripts occur without the cross-origin attribute, a common issue with scripts. I\'m focusing on the interaction of error handling and cross-origin policies.\n\n\n**Investigating Registration Failures**\n\nI\'m now digging into scenarios that lead to service worker registration failures, particularly within iframes. I suspect a sandbox issue might cause the browser to abort registrations, which then manifests as the generic \"Script error.\" I\'m looking at how these benign cancellations are misconstrued as application errors. I\'m also examining the effects of the `crossorigin` attribute in the `index.html` file and how it might play a role. I\'m checking the `useVoiceTyping.ts` hook for other clues.\n\n\n**Refining Error Handling**\n\nI\'m now refining the error handling related to speech recognition and synthesis. I\'ve updated the `useVoiceTyping.ts` hook to ensure `abort()` is called safely and to handle specific `onerror` cases gracefully. I\'m also addressing errors like \'aborted\' and \'no-speech\' without throwing or setting an error state. I\'ve also checked `AIVoiceNewsPlayer.tsx` for similar error considerations around speech synthesis cancellation and updated `LiveWeatherService.ts` to include robust try/catch blocks for handling aborted fetches or network failures, preventing unhandled rejections.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 2
      nanos: 878854495
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
